    "problem": "The paper addresses why the expected Northern European winter cooling from an intensified negative North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO-), driven by Arctic sea ice loss, is 'missing' in simulations, as this dynamical cooling is offset by thermodynamical warming from the advection of warmer air masses.",
    "method": "This study uses large-ensemble atmospheric simulations with prescribed sea ice conditions to examine how negative North Atlantic Oscillation events are affected, partitioning the resulting temperature response into dynamical (circulation-driven) and thermodynamical (air mass warming) components to explain the absence of expected cooling.",
    "task": "This study uses large-ensemble atmospheric simulations to examine why Arctic sea ice loss, despite intensifying the negative North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO-), does not cause the expected winter cooling over Northern Europe, finding that warming from advected air masses offsets the dynamical cooling effect.",
